                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "As a longtime fan of the prequel, I'd love to give this game a positive review. Yet in its present state, I simply couldn't do so in good faith for the following reasons. \n\nFirstly, the in game has a steep learning curve. Steeper in practice than it should be due to an unintuitive UI, lack of in-game tutorials and online informationals. The current in game tutorial is only a few drop downs. That's enough for simple games-but not this one. There is a wiki, but its an outdated 13 page stub of a wiki. The new-player-help channel in the discord community is a great idea, but is a far cry from the convenience a quick google search or in-game guidance could be providing mid-gaming session.\n\nSecondly, the single EU server is hardly ever active. This is noted by many other negative reviews here–and I'll reiterate the general sentiment of that: the game simply isn't all that fun to play alone (the name of the game is territory control after all!). So why aren't people playing then?\n\nThere appear to be a few interactions between the game's pacing, player moderation and server management that are contributing to the problem. The game has a very gradual pacing. It takes time to mine, build, craft and fortify your base. Long enough-especially for new players that one can expect a team to collectively log off before they get to combat. After all, most people have lives to live outside of gaming. And, this wouldn't be an issue at face value given the long intervals between server wipes. Ideally, you could take a few days off and come back to your base ready to experience the core combat mechanics of the game having built up your infrastructures in prior sessions. Yet after many hours of play, I've yet to really get that experience and here's why:\n\nOther players will offline you after a single session of base building. This demands another 1-2 gaming sessions to recoup your losses, only to have the whole cycle of offlining repeat again while you touch grass (as healthy gamers do). Now many other games of this kind have to deal with offlining, but here's the thing: the game and its server moderators don't currently do anything to manage it. No kicks, no warnings, no alternative servers. It just happens and people disengage or leave the community outright because of it.\n\nThere is a second major contributing factor to the low player retention rates, but I'll only mention it briefly–simply because reviewers with more hours cover the details of it in greater depth. The server wipes are very inconsistent, sometimes not happening for really long periods of time. This matters because it puts the only server into a perpetual state of atrophy as certain resources are severely depleted making it hard to do anything productive in-game.\n\nIn summary, I think the game could really benefit from some combination of improved changes to the pacing or the addition of consistently active alternative game modes, scheduled server wipes, in game moderation to manage offlining, robust in game tutorials (and online informationals), a cleanup of the current UI/crafting menus and better (or at least better explained) damage/health systems.",
